# DevLab backend (devlabd) — systemd unit TEMPLATE.
#
# Instance values (domain, cookie domain, ports, run user, GitHub owner) belong in a local
# drop-in (/etc/systemd/system/devlabd.service.d/*.conf), NEVER in this repo. The values below
# are overridable defaults. There is NO operating-mode variable — modes do not exist
# (REQ-027.1); DEVLAB_RUNS_MAX_CONCURRENCY, when set, is only the FIRST-start seed of the
# runtime settings pool (REQ-013.2).
#
# Environment-contract migration (B-45 — one note per changed variable):
#   DEVLAB_GH_OWNER           now MANDATORY, previously optional. The GitHub owner the managed
#                             repository set lives under has NO default any more: a default would
#                             scope both the set and every repository the delivery chain creates
#                             to another organisation. Unset, every resolution fails closed with
#                             an empty set and the named ErrOwnerUnset. Set it in the drop-in —
#                             it is an instance value and therefore not in this template.
#   DEVLAB_RUNS_MODE          REMOVED. Operating modes do not exist; the one chain always runs
#                             preflight → implement → deliver-dev → publish → pull-request.
#   DEVLAB_RUNS_MAX_COST*     REMOVED. There is no cost cap (REQ-017): consumption is counted
#                             live and reported, never capped. The time budget is a per-run
#                             tunable in the runtime settings, not an env variable.
#   DEVLAB_RUNS_AUTOMERGE     RENAMED to DEVLAB_RUNS_AUTOMERGE_WINDOW, and demoted: it SEEDS the
#                             runtime settings pool on the first start only (REQ-013.2), so the
#                             stored window wins from the first write on. The old name is read by
#                             nothing — left in a drop-in it silently does nothing.
#   DEVLAB_RUNS_LIMIT_MAXRESUMES
#                             RENAMED to DEVLAB_RUNS_LIMIT_MAX_RESUMES (one word boundary, so the
#                             name matches every other DEVLAB_RUNS_* knob). Read by nothing under
#                             the old spelling: a usage-limit cap left behind would silently become
#                             the default of 8 resumes.
#   DEVLAB_RUNS_AGENT_TIMEOUT REMOVED. The per-attempt wall-clock budget is the settings pool's
#                             DefaultTimeBudget, seeded once by DEVLAB_RUNS_TIME_BUDGET and tuned
#                             per run afterwards (REQ-010); DEVLAB_RUNS_MAX_DURATION remains the
#                             scheduler's hard stop per attempt.
#   DEVLAB_RUNS_PROTECTION_START_DELAY / DEVLAB_RUNS_PROTECTION_ENFORCE
#                             NEW. The branch-protection pass reaches every repository of the
#                             configured organisation, so it is held: the first pass waits (default
#                             15m) instead of running at boot, and it only READS and REPORTS until
#                             the operator arms writing. See the hardening block below.
#   DEVLAB_RUNS_MAINTAIN_ENFORCE
#                             NEW, and the second switch of the same kind. The recurring pull-request
#                             maintenance merges, prunes delivery branches and writes commit statuses
#                             in the managed repositories, driven by the scheduler tick — so on a
#                             freshly migrated instance it would do all of that from the FIRST tick,
#                             over a pool nobody has looked at yet. Unarmed (the default) the pass
#                             reports the standstill from DevLab's own pools and makes no foreign call
#                             at all. Arm it in the drop-in once the pool has been inspected
#                             (00-cutover.md step 6a).
#   run-active/runs-active    REMOVED (REQ-039.3). Activity is a projection over the execution
#                             documents; the only marker file is mercury/restart.json.
#   DEVLAB_LINKS, DEVLAB_WORKSPACES, DEVLAB_STATIC_DIR, DEVLAB_REPOS_PATH
#                             now DERIVE from DEVLAB_STATE_DIR (internal/statepath). The
#                             historical per-variable overrides keep working as overrides for
#                             genuinely split roots; stop setting them otherwise. DEVLAB_REPOS_PATH
#                             in particular is only the dev-bypass sandbox base: carried over from an
#                             older unit that pointed it at an operator home, it aims repository
#                             resolution at foreign working copies instead of the per-user
#                             workspaces under the state root.
#   listen address            DEVLAB_ADDR ONLY. The binary parses NO command-line flags, so a
#                             `--listen <addr>` argument carried over from an older ExecStart is
#                             silently ignored and the process listens wherever DEVLAB_ADDR points
#                             — which must be the address the edge proxies to.
#   DEVLAB_COOKIE_DOMAIN      unchanged in meaning, but it is an INSTANCE value and therefore no
#                             longer in this template (see the commented line below). Unset, the
#                             session cookies this service re-mints are host-only and stand BESIDE
#                             the landscape's domain-wide ones instead of overwriting them.
#   DEVLAB_RESTART_POLL / DEVLAB_RESTART_MAXWAIT
#                             unchanged, but now consumed by devlab-restart-when-free polling
#                             the ready SOCKET (restart-ready.sock) — the PID busy-marker of
#                             devlab-restart-idle is gone.
#
# Install (once, as root — the `service` CLI performs these steps interactively):
#   sudo install -m0755 devlabd /usr/local/bin/devlabd
#   sudo install -m0644 deploy/devlabd.service /etc/systemd/system/devlabd.service
#   sudo install -m0644 permissions/devlab.json /etc/holistic/permissions.d/devlab.json
#   sudo groupadd -f hp_devlab_access
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl enable --now devlabd

[Unit]
Description=DevLab backend (devlabd)
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
# Dedicated unprivileged service user. Per-user git/file ops (and terminal/AI) run AS the
# logged-in Linux user via the pinned devlab-exec sudo wrapper; the service itself only reads.
User=devlab
# holistic: the shared landscape group (jwt-secret, revoked.json). systemd-journal: the honest
# running gate (deploy.Gate) reads a just-delivered unit's OWN journal to name WHY a failed start
# died; without this group journalctl answers "No journal files were opened due to insufficient
# permissions", and that gate deficiency was mistaken for the service's failure reason (2026-08-08,
# holistic-dashboard booked as failed although it was live). The group is read-only — it grants the
# service nothing but the ability to READ the system journal.
SupplementaryGroups=holistic systemd-journal
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/devlabd
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=3
# On SIGTERM devlabd drains: admissions close, agent children get SIGTERM, every running
# execution is persisted as interrupted with its continuation (< DEVLAB_RUNS_DRAIN_GRACE),
# then HTTP shuts down. Keep TimeoutStopSec above that grace (K-2) — a long run never blocks
# the stop.
TimeoutStopSec=90

# The ONE state root. Every persisted path derives from it (internal/statepath).
Environment=DEVLAB_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/devlab
# Listen address (loopback; the edge proxy routes the public host here).
Environment=DEVLAB_ADDR=127.0.0.1:8781
# Shared Holistic session secret (validates the h_access SSO cookie). Missing/unreadable
# secret makes devlabd refuse to start in SSO mode (fail closed).
Environment=HOLISTIC_SECRET_FILE=/etc/holistic/jwt-secret
Environment=DEVLAB_COOKIE_SECURE=1
# The shared parent domain the landscape's session cookies are scoped to. It is an instance value,
# so it is set in the drop-in and stays out of this template; unset, the cookies re-minted here are
# host-only and shadow the domain-wide ones instead of overwriting them:
#   Environment=DEVLAB_COOKIE_DOMAIN=<cookie-domain>
# Honor a Holistic logout/rotation at refresh time (read-only).
Environment=HOLISTIC_REVOKED=/var/lib/holistic/revoked.json
# Per-user GitHub link store key + OAuth app config (operator-provisioned files).
Environment=DEVLAB_LINK_ENC_KEY_FILE=/etc/devlab/link-key
Environment=DEVLAB_GITHUB_OAUTH_FILE=/etc/devlab/github-oauth.json
# MANDATORY, no default: the GitHub owner whose topic-marked repositories form the managed set.
# It is an instance value, so it is set in the drop-in and stays out of this template:
#   Environment=DEVLAB_GH_OWNER=<github-owner>
# The set marker itself does have a default ("holistic", the landscape-wide topic); override it
# only where the instance marks membership differently:
#   Environment=DEVLAB_GH_TOPIC=<topic>
# Drain/resume knobs (runtime settings win over env where a settings value exists).
Environment=DEVLAB_RUNS_DRAIN_GRACE=60s
Environment=DEVLAB_RUNS_RESUME_WINDOW=240h
# Branch protection (REQ-033.7) is the ONE recurring pass whose effect leaves DevLab: it walks every
# repository of the configured organisation and, armed, PATCHes the default branch of each one that
# deviates. So it is held by default — the first pass waits, and an unarmed pass reads and reports
# without changing anything. Arm it in the drop-in once the reported findings are the intended ones:
#   Environment=DEVLAB_RUNS_PROTECTION_ENFORCE=1
Environment=DEVLAB_RUNS_PROTECTION_START_DELAY=15m
# The pull-request maintenance is the SECOND pass whose effect leaves DevLab: it merges, prunes
# delivery branches and writes commit statuses in the managed repositories, on the scheduler's tick.
# It is held the same way — unarmed it reports the standstill and makes no foreign call — and it is
# armed in the drop-in once the pending-pull-request pool has been inspected:
#   Environment=DEVLAB_RUNS_MAINTAIN_ENFORCE=1

# Hardening. NoNewPrivileges must be false (the service escalates via the pinned sudo
# wrappers to run ops as the end user); ProtectHome false so per-user git/claude configs
# resolve. Isolation is at the sudo/OS-user layer (devlab-exec), not the mount namespace.
StateDirectory=devlab
# 0711, not systemd's default 0755: the state root holds the link store, the transcripts and the
# restart-ready socket, so no local account may LIST it — but the per-user workspaces below it are
# entered by devlab-exec running AS the end user, which needs the traverse bit (x). 0750 would cut
# that path and break every per-user git op. The workspaces root keeps the same shape (0711).
StateDirectoryMode=0711
# 0027: what the daemon creates is readable by itself and the operating group only, never by every
# local user. It matches devlab-exec's umask, so a file's mode does not depend on who created it.
# Security-relevant modes are still set explicitly at the site (e.g. api.SocketMode) — this is the
# floor, never the guarantee.
UMask=0027
# ProtectSystem=strict makes the WHOLE hierarchy read-only apart from what is listed here — /tmp
# included. Every per-user child this service starts through devlab-exec needs a writable one: the
# claude CLI keeps its scratch in /tmp/claude-<uid>, and `go build` / `npm run build` put their work
# there. So /tmp is writable explicitly rather than through PrivateTmp: a private /tmp would give the
# service a namespace of its own, where the per-user scratch is invisible to the user's own session and
# to every other service, while the isolation this service actually relies on is the OS user
# (devlab-exec), not the mount namespace. A drop-in that sets PrivateTmp=true silently takes that
# decision back — the cutover removes it.
#
# The four install targets are writable for ONE reason: the mount namespace is inherited by every
# child, the pinned root installer (devlab-install) is such a child, and a read-only /opt made EVERY
# dev deploy of EVERY service fail with "chmod failed with error Read-only file system". Measured on
# 2026-07-30: nine services in one execution, all with that one sentence.
#
# It gives the daemon itself nothing: it runs as the unprivileged `devlab` account, and all four
# paths are root-owned, so file permissions still refuse it. What the namespace was blocking was the
# root child alone — and the boundary this service actually relies on is the sudoers pin plus the OS
# user (devlab-exec), as stated above, not the mount namespace. Exactly the paths the installer
# writes are listed, nothing wider:
#   /opt                  the service artifact (/opt/<id>/bin/<id>d)
#   /usr/local/bin        the self repository's own binary
#   /etc/systemd/system   the generated unit of a first-time setup
#   /etc/caddy/conf.d     its generated route
#   /var/lib              the state directory of a first-time setup (useradd creates /var/lib/<id>)
#
# DELIBERATELY OUT OF THIS LIST — reached OUTSIDE the cgroup, not by widening the sandbox. Some things
# the chain must write are NOT here on purpose, because giving the service standing write to them would
# let it do — unattended — the very thing a human is meant to approve. ProtectSystem=strict makes every
# path outside this list read-only for the service AND every child it starts, so these are reached only
# by handing the one approved operation OUT of the cgroup into a transient systemd unit (systemd-run),
# exactly as the self-repo restart does (deploy/devlab-install --handover):
#   /usr/local/sbin       the root wrappers themselves. Listed here, the service could rewrite its own
#                         root scripts and so grant itself root without approval — the whole point of
#                         keeping it out. An approved renewal writes it via the hand-out in
#                         deploy/devlab-install (maybe_hand_out_renewal). Before that existed, an approved
#                         renewal hit "mktemp: Read-only file system" and the same question came back in a
#                         loop (measured 2026-08-06).
# KNOWN, NOT YET CLOSED — the SAME class, named so nobody rediscovers it by running into it (task point
# 5). First-time setup of a FOREIGN service writes three paths that are ALSO read-only here, so it would
# fail the same way the wrapper renewal did, and the right fix is the same hand-out, never adding these to
# the list (that would hand the service standing write over system identity and rights):
#   /etc/passwd,/etc/shadow,/etc/group   useradd/groupadd for the new service account (setup_ensure_account)
#   /etc/holistic/permissions.d          the service's rights manifest copied in at first-time setup
# Two more paths must lie UNDER a writable root above (they are instance configuration, not literals
# here): the holistic dashboard checkout and its serve root, which the confined ui rebuild/serve writes —
# place them under /opt or /var/lib, or that step fails read-only the same way.
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib /tmp /opt /usr/local/bin /etc/systemd/system /etc/caddy/conf.d
NoNewPrivileges=false
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=false
PrivateTmp=false

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
